```c
alt_dht->wrap = dht->used;
...
new = 0;
addr = alt_dht->size;
if (dht->used) {
    old = hpack_dht_get_tail(dht);
    do {
        alt_dht->dte[new].nlen = dht->dte[old].nlen;
        alt_dht->dte[new].vlen = dht->dte[old].vlen;
        addr -= dht->dte[old].nlen + dht->dte[old].vlen;
        alt_dht->dte[new].addr = addr;
        memcpy((void *)alt_dht + alt_dht->dte[new].addr,
               (void *)dht + dht->dte[old].addr,
               dht->dte[old].nlen + dht->dte[old].vlen);
        old++;
        if (old >= dht->wrap)
            old = 0;
        new++;
    } while (new < dht->used);
}
alt_dht->front = alt_dht->head = new - 1;
memcpy(dht, alt_dht, dht->size);
hpack_dht_free(alt_dht);
return dht;
```

Here, `alt_dht->wrap = dht->used`. But `alt_dht` is allocated with `pool->size` = 4096 bytes. The `dte` array is at the start. `used` entries of 8 bytes each. So `used * 8 + sizeof(*dht) <= 4096` must hold. 

But wait - `dht->used` could be large. Let me check the bound. From `hpack_dht_make_room`:
```c
if (dht->used * 32 + dht->total + needed + 32 <= dht->size)
    return 1;
```
So `dht->used * 32 <= dht->size = 4096`, so `dht->used <= 128`. And `128 * 8 + sizeof(*dht) = 1024 + 16 = 1040 <= 4096`. So that's fine.

